  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential relationship between Norleucine and the Inner centromere protein, mediated through Calmodulin. Norleucine is identified as a target of Calmodulin in multiple pathways, with Calmodulin acting as a central hub in each of the three provided evidence chains. In each case, Calmodulin is a target of a different compound (e.g., (1R,6R,9S,13R)-6-hydroxy-5,5,9,14-tetramethyltetracyclo[11.2.1.01,10.04,9]hexadecan-12-one, Hemerocallone, and Br-Xanthone A), all of which are linked to the Inner centromere protein. The scores for these pathways are consistent, indicating a plausible biological relationship. The shared involvement of Calmodulin and the presence of multiple compounds targeting it suggest a possible mechanism by which Norleucine may influence the Inner centromere protein. However, the evidence is limited to these specific pathways, and further experimental validation would be required to confirm the direct relationship.",
